 <section xml:id="Section-2">
        <title>Gaussian Elimination</title>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>By applying row operations, any matrix may be written in reduced row echelon form. Furthermore, the reduced row echelon form of that matrix is unique.</p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>The <term>Gaussian Elimination</term> algorithm goes as follows:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Pick a row <m>A_{i}</m> with leftmost leading entry.</p></li>
        <li><p>Scale that row so that its leading entry is <m>1</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>For each row <m>A_{j}</m> with the same leading column, replace it with <m>A_{j} - \ell A_{i}</m>, where <m>\ell</m> is the leading entry of <m>A_{j}</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Apply interchange to move <m>A_{i}</m> to the top row</p></li>
        <li><p>Repeat on the submatrix consisting of all rows other than <m>A_{i}</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>For each pivot column, apply replacements to make all entry other than the unique row with leading entry in that pivot column equal to <m>0</m>.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <p>Let's show this on an example. Consider the linear system:</p>
      <me>\begin{aligned}
        4x + 2y &amp;= 0,\\
        2x &amp;= 4,\\
        y &amp;= 8.
      \end{aligned}</me>

      <p>Start with the following matrix, which is the augmented matrix of the system:</p>
      <me>\begin{pmatrix}
        4 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 \\
        2 &amp; 0 &amp; 4 \\
        0 &amp; 1 &amp; 8
      \end{pmatrix}</me>

      <p>Pick a row with leftmost leading entry such as the second row. Rescale that row so its first entry is <m>1</m>.</p>
      <me>\begin{pmatrix}
        4 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 \\
        1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 \\
        0 &amp; 1 &amp; 8
      \end{pmatrix}</me>

      <p>Then use it to cancel out the leading entry of any other row with the same leading entry by using replacement.</p>
      <me>\begin{pmatrix}
        0 &amp; 2 &amp; -8 \\
        1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 \\
        0 &amp; 1 &amp; 8
      \end{pmatrix}</me>

      <p>Use interchange to place that row as the first row in the matrix.</p>
      <me>\begin{pmatrix}
        1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 \\
        0 &amp; 2 &amp; -8 \\
        0 &amp; 1 &amp; 8
      \end{pmatrix}</me>

      <p>Then follow the same strategy on the remaining rows. The bottom row already has first nonzero entry equal to <m>1</m>, so we can take that one. We replace the second row with it minus twice the third row.</p>
      <me>\begin{pmatrix}
        1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 \\
        0 &amp; 0 &amp; -24 \\
        0 &amp; 1 &amp; 8
      \end{pmatrix}</me>

      <p>Swap until row <m>3</m> is at the top of the rows not already sorted, so we swap it with row <m>2</m>.</p>
      <me>\begin{pmatrix}
        1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 \\
        0 &amp; 1 &amp; 8\\
        0 &amp; 0 &amp; -24
      \end{pmatrix}</me>

      <p>Then we have one more row. We rescale it to be <m>1</m>. Then we cancel out the entries in the columns for that row via replacement. In the end we get the identity matrix.</p>
      <me>\begin{pmatrix}
        1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\
        0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\
        0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1
      \end{pmatrix}</me>

      <p>Putting this back into equational form we get</p>
      <me>\begin{aligned}
        x &amp;= 0 \\
        y &amp;= 0 \\
        0 &amp;= 1
      \end{aligned}</me>

      <p>Thus, the system has no solutions, since <m>0 \neq 1</m>. In fact, the certification of there being no solution is an example of a more general phenomenon:</p>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>A linear system is consistent if and only if the final column of the reduced row echelon form of its augmented matrix is not a pivot column.</p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
    </section>